    "body": "## Summary\n\nFinal commit of ADR-059. **First measurable perf win of the session.**\n\n## The verified delta (Release, 3 fresh Wikipedia loads)\n\n| Run | Cascade | Speedup vs 1058ms |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 200.3ms | 5.3× |\n| 2 | 188.7ms | 5.6× |\n| 3 | 189.6ms | 5.6× |\n\n**~190ms stable, 5.6× structural speedup.** Saves ~870ms per Wikipedia page load. Cascade share of render time: **31% → 6-7%**.\n\n## The layout variance discovery\n\nLayout (unchanged by ADR-059 — same code path as pre) showed **3× variance across the same 3 runs**:\n\n| Run | Layout |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | 2750ms |\n| 2 | 2415ms |\n| 3 | 7156ms |\n\nIdentical content, identical binary. Environmental noise on the 8GB test machine (Firefox ~900MB resident, memory pressure). **Not a regression from ADR-059.**\n\n**Methodology lesson for ADR-060**: layout perf requires median-of-N multi-run sampling, not single runs. Small-page control confirms measurement path is sound (`docs/demo.html`: cascade 0.1ms, layout 23ms first, 0.5-1.3ms subsequent).\n\n## Two implementation bugs caught before shipping\n\n1. **Namespace-scope bug**: first `build_index` placement was appended after the closing `} // namespace` — compiled cleanly as a free function but `Stylesheet` unreachable. 22 errors, all same root. Fix: relocated block inside the namespace.\n\n2. **Test literal `\\n`**: Python's `'\\\\n'` in a heredoc appended literal `\\` + `n` chars to `test_stylesheet.cpp` instead of a newline. One-line fix.\n\n**Same class as ADR-057's definition-body bug and ADR-058's `ctx` scope bug**: patch the symbol where it's *used*, not just *declared*. Compiler catches the mismatch immediately — but only if the whole project is rebuilt (the first test run used the old binary and showed stale results).\n\n## Why the anchor priority is a real invariant\n\n`anchor_for` picks `Id > Class > Type > Universal`. Each is a **mandatory match**: if the element doesn't have the specific id/class/tag, the selector cannot match. Bucketing by the most selective gives the smallest bucket without ever dropping a should-match rule.\n\n**The bug class this protects against**: any mandatory-match simple works for correctness. Priority picks the *smallest* bucket, not correctness. The real bug class is *missing* anchors — attribute-only rules (`[href]`, `[aria-expanded]`) fall into universal. If universal grows large, index saves less. Universal bucket size should be measured as follow-up if it's >500 rules on Wikipedia.\n\n## What this PR carries\n\n- ADR-059 → Implemented (5.6× cascade, 3-run stable, Release)\n- Post-ADR-059 note in ADR-058 (split-timer enabled the fix; methodology lesson)\n- Post-ADR-059 note in ADR-022 (threshold design held up; ~17 → ~5 baseline)\n- README row + CHANGELOG entry\n\n## Deferrals documented\n\n- **Pseudo-target partitioning** (3× memory, not needed at ~190ms)\n- **`[attribute]` indexing** (follow-up ADR-059b if universal bucket >500 rules)\n- **Descendant combinator ancestor pre-filtering**\n- **Layout's 2.4s** — ADR-060 territory, needs multi-run methodology\n\n## Test count\n\n- 675 → 680 cases (+5)\n- 2586 → 2608 assertions (+22)\n- **Zero flips** on the 3 ADR-022 counter tests (`< 100` bound already covered both baselines)\n- Documentation only; no code changes in this PR\n\nRefs #258\n",
